# ─── VENDOR FIRMWARE BLOBS - never in this repository (docs/wifi.md 8) ────────
#
# Some devices are not fixed-function silicon. The Pi 4's CYW43455 radio contains its own processor with
# no ROM firmware for the MAC, so it does nothing until a host uploads Broadcom's binary into it. That
# binary is not driver code anyone writes - not Linux, not the Pi's firmware, not Windows - and its
# licence permits redistributing it while explicitly forbidding any attempt to "modify in any way,
# reverse engineer, decompile or disassemble" it.
#
# THE DECISION, which is Linux's: the OS tree carries no blobs. Linux moved theirs out years ago into a
# separate `linux-firmware` repository that distributions package (Debian ships `non-free-firmware`; the
# Pi set is `RPi-Distro/firmware-nonfree`), and the driver reads the file off the FILESYSTEM at runtime.
# Here the blob lives on the machine's own data disk, supplied by whoever owns the machine, and
# `wifi-driver` opens it through `fs` like any other client - no kernel involvement at all, which is one
# place this architecture is simpler than Linux's `request_firmware()` callback.
#
# A decision in a document is honoured by whoever read it; a pattern here is honoured by everyone.
# `git add -f` still works, which is the right shape: possible deliberately, never by accident.
*.clm_blob
brcmfmac*.bin
brcmfmac*.txt
rtl8*fw*.bin
firmware/**/*.bin
